UID:hva-digital-life-researcher-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260730
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260731
SUMMARY:Deadline: Amsterdam University of Applied Sciences: Digital Life Re
 searcher (Embodied AI Rehabilitation Agents)
DESCRIPTION:A junior researcher post in the Digital Life research group at 
 the Amsterdam University of Applied Sciences\, working on embodied AI reha
 bilitation agents that support patients during rehabilitation and recovery
  in everyday life. The programme sits at the intersection of human-centred
  AI\, digital health and rehabilitation technology\, and the role is unusu
 ally hands-on for a research job: co-designing rehabilitation technologies
  with patients and healthcare professionals\, building interactive prototy
 pes with sensing technologies and embodied interaction\, running user-cent
 red evaluations in real-world living lab settings\, and contributing to sc
 ientific reports and presentations. REQUIREMENTS: a master's degree in hum
 an-computer interaction\, industrial design\, computer science\, software 
 engineering\, AI or a related field\; demonstrated research experience inc
 luding co-design and evaluation with diverse stakeholders\; experience des
 igning interactive prototypes with sensing or embodied systems\; sensor da
 ta collection\, processing and analysis skills\; and programming in Python
 \, JavaScript or comparable languages. Excellent written and spoken Englis
 h is required and Dutch proficiency is preferred. Apply with a CV and moti
 vation letter through the application portal. Contact: Bin Yu (b.yu@hva.nl
 \, 0638347028) or vacatures@hva.nl. Closes 30 July 2026.\n\nOrganisation: 
 Amsterdam University of Applied Sciences (Hogeschool van Amsterdam)\nLocat
 ion: Amsterdam\, Netherlands (Weesperzijde campus)\, on site\nAward: EUR 3
 \,064.08 to 4\,084.93 gross per month for 40 hours per week\, plus an 8.3 
 percent year-end bonus and 8 percent holiday allowance.\nDuration: Full-ti
 me\, 40 hours per week\n\nMore: https://www.academictransfer.com/en/jobs/3
 62580/digital-life-onderzoeksmedewerker-researcher/?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.academictransfer.com/en/jobs/362580/digital-life-onderzoeks
 medewerker-researcher/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
LOCATION:Amsterdam\, Netherlands (Weesperzijde campus)\, on site
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:glina3-open-call-type-language-visual-systems-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260812
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260813
SUMMARY:Deadline: GLINA3: Open Call for a Collaborator Working with Type\, 
 Language and Visual Systems
DESCRIPTION:GLINA3 is an Amsterdam clay 3D printing and digital design stud
 io\, and it is looking for one collaborator to join a new research project
  at the intersection of typography\, language\, technology and material cu
 lture. They want an artist\, writer\, poet\, anthropologist or artistic re
 searcher whose existing practice already engages with type design\, typogr
 aphy\, writing systems\, visual language\, fonts and letterforms\, languag
 e technologies\, or the tools that shape how language is produced\, read o
 r translated. This is an unusually good fit for anyone working on the mach
 ine-readable side of language: the brief explicitly names language technol
 ogies and translation tooling alongside classical type design. ELIGIBILITY
 : individual practitioners with demonstrable work in one or more of the li
 sted areas. HOW TO APPLY: the studio asks for relevant work\, research\, p
 ublications or projects connected to type\, fonts\, writing systems\, visu
 al language or the tools that shape them\, and nothing else. Because the d
 eadline is tight they say Instagram DM is the fastest route\; you can also
  email a short introduction and one relevant project to office@glina3.nl. 
 NOTE: the fee is confirmed only if the project's own funding comes through
 \, so clarify terms before committing time.\n\nOrganisation: GLINA3\nLocat
 ion: Amsterdam\, Netherlands (studio is Amsterdam-based\; collaboration te
 rms not specified)\nAward: Paid collaboration. The studio states the fee w
 ill be confirmed upon successful funding\, so the amount is contingent and
  not published up front. No application fee.\nDuration: New collaborative 
 research project\, length not specified\n\nMore: https://www.instagram.com
 /p/Dbse-4jDNj5/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.instagram.com/p/Dbse-4jDNj5/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Amsterdam\, Netherlands (studio is Amsterdam-based\; collaboration
  terms not specified)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:foodculture-days-kitchen-central-location-2027@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Kitchen / Central Location Open Call (foodculture day
 s Biennale\, Vevey 2027)
DESCRIPTION:foodculture days is opening up The Kitchen / Central Location\,
  the living heart of its biennale in Vevey\, to an outside collective for 
 the festival's 6th edition. The Kitchen is at once an open-air kitchen\, a
  gathering space and an artistic installation\, sited by the lake on the E
 splanade de la Paix along a busy promenade\, hosting multidisciplinary pro
 jects\, concerts\, themed meals\, public discussions\, citizens' assemblie
 s and moments of collective cooking. The call asks for new ways of inhabit
 ing it. WHO THEY WANT: a transdisciplinary collective of scientists\, arti
 sts\, designers\, craftspeople\, cooks\, activists and architects\, either
  newly formed or already active\; collaboration across generations\, cultu
 res and levels of expertise is encouraged. WHAT IS OFFERED: an open-air ci
 tizen experimentation laboratory in Vevey's public space\, framed around r
 ethinking ways of living and the relationship to time and to creation\, an
 d thinking together about the conditions under which multidisciplinary pra
 ctices can flourish. THE BRIEF: kitchen and hospitality\, lunch and dinner
  service\, vegetarian meals with a vegan option. Meals should be affordabl
 e\, seasonal where fresh produce is involved\, and conceived in the spirit
  of a communal canteen: simple\, generous\, nourishing and full of flavour
 . Expected covers are roughly 100 guests Wednesday to Thursday and roughly
  150 Friday to Sunday. WHAT THE FESTIVAL PROVIDES: curatorial support to d
 evelop the proposal\, with particular attention to the themed meals\; intr
 oductions to local producers\, growers and artisans including farm visits 
 and foraging sites\; production support and volunteers throughout\; and st
 andard professional kitchen equipment and cooking facilities. BUDGET: CHF 
 15\,000 all-inclusive (VAT included) covering travel\, visas\, equipment r
 ental\, any additional kitchen equipment\, per diems\, materials and artis
 t fees\; food costs and accommodation in Vevey are covered separately. THE
  SPACE: the pavilion\, called Cuisine\, is a modular metal structure assem
 bled from repurposed agricultural greenhouse components on a 3.1 metre bid
 irectional grid\, with pillars and arches forming ridge vaults\, a textile
  covering naturally dyed yellow with reseda\, and a terracotta brick heart
 h for wood-fired cooking laid directly on the floor. It comes with dining 
 tables\, low tables\, a reception table\, a shelf\, a bar\, pendant lights
 \, 30 cushions\, removable curtains\, flags\, blackboards and signage pane
 ls\, and can expand\, contract or be reconfigured. TIMELINE: Phase 1 deadl
 ine 31 August 2026 at midnight CET\; three shortlisted proposals selected 
 in late September\, each invited to an online meeting and paid a CHF 250 r
 esearch fee for Phase 2\; final project selected and announced in early Oc
 tober 2026\; collaborative development with the foodculture days architect
 ural team October to November\; production December to February\; build an
 d installation in Vevey 1 to 16 June 2027\, with the collective present th
 roughout those two weeks\; 6th edition of the biennale 16 to 20 June 2027.
  PHASE 1 QUESTIONS: tell us about your collective (origins\, intention\, w
 hat makes you special)\; member biographies with collective or individual 
 portfolios\, website\, Instagram\, city of residence and practices\; what 
 the concept consists of in terms of food and cuisine\, relationships\, and
  scenography and spatial research\; what you want to put into practice in 
 the central pavilion\, your vision and the kind of experience you want to 
 create\; any special requirements\; and references\, including a quote rep
 resenting the collective\, two reference texts\, three images and optional
 ly video. An Open Call Information Kit\, a recording of the online informa
 tion session and a Q&A are published on the open call site and are worth r
 eading before applying.\n\nOrganisation: foodculture days\nLocation: Vevey
 \, Switzerland. The Kitchen is built on the Esplanade de la Paix by the la
 keside. The selected collective must be present in Vevey for the two-week 
 build and installation\, 1 to 16 June 2027\, and through the biennale 16 t
 o 20 June 2027.\nAward: CHF 15\,000 all-inclusive project budget (VAT incl
 uded)\, covering travel\, visas\, equipment and additional kitchen equipme
 nt rental\, per diems\, materials and artist fees. Food costs and accommod
 ation in Vevey are covered separately by foodculture days. Three shortlist
 ed collectives each receive a CHF 250 research fee to develop their propos
 al in Phase 2. One collective is selected. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 October 2026 to June 2027: collaborative development October-November\, pr
 oduction December-February\, on-site build 1-16 June 2027\, biennale 16-20
  June 2027\n\nMore: https://opencall.foodculturedays.com/en?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://opencall.foodculturedays.com/en?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Vevey\, Switzerland. The Kitchen is built on the Esplanade de la P
 aix by the lakeside. The selected collective must be present in Vevey for 
 the two-week build and installation\, 1 to 16 June 2027\, and through the 
 biennale 16 to 20 June 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:impart-nalli-fellowships-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Impart & Nalli Fellowships 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:A research fellowship from Impart\, an online platform building
  accessible resources on South Asia's art histories\, in collaboration wit
 h Nalli Silks\, a heritage textile brand established in 1928. The programm
 e has supported two research projects a year since 2024-25\; for the 2027-
 28 cycle two fellowships are awarded\, each carrying a grant of up to INR 
 5.5 lakhs for one year of independent research and project development. AR
 EAS OF FOCUS: underexplored textile-related histories\, practices and trad
 itions across South Asia. Any textile-centric subject may be proposed\; th
 e call particularly encourages textiles in religious\, literary\, liturgic
 al and mythological contexts\; the environmental impact of textile product
 ion and consumption\; endangered or disappearing textile traditions\; crit
 ical perspectives on textile revivalism\, digitisation\, archives and mate
 rial afterlives\; labour in textile production with attention to caste\, c
 lass and gender\; relationships between textiles and other cultural forms 
 including storytelling\, poetry\, music\, architecture and material cultur
 e\; techniques\, technologies and tools tied to specific traditions\; Sout
 h Asian textiles within the contemporary global design economy\; intellect
 ual property\, ownership and cultural rights\; and socio-cultural perspect
 ives on the relationship between handmade and industrial textiles. ELIGIBI
 LITY: open to anyone aged 18 or over residing anywhere in the world\, prov
 ided the project engages with textile practices in the wider South Asia re
 gion (Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\,
  Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka\, Tibet). Early- and mid-career researchers\
 , designers\, archivists\, journalists\, writers\, educators\, practitione
 rs\, collectives and small organisations are all welcome\, and collaborati
 ve projects are permitted provided co-creators are credited and listed in 
 the application. Both new and ongoing projects are eligible\, but applican
 ts must specify which part of the project the grant will develop and discl
 ose any current or previous funding from other organisations. SUPPORTED FO
 RMATS: journalistic inquiry\, documentary film\, archive activation\, prac
 tice-based research\, archive generation\, digitisation and documentation 
 initiatives\, and exhibition or book projects. DELIVERABLES: a comprehensi
 ve project report at the end of the grant period\; a research dossier deve
 loped from that report in collaboration with Impart\, presenting key quest
 ions and findings and hosted on Impart's website for public benefit\; and 
 a set of final project outputs\, which may be audio-visual (interviews\, s
 hort films)\, visual essays (photographic or process-based documentation)\
 , written pieces (essays\, features\, interviews) or curatorial work (exhi
 bition or digital curation). Fellows also take part in periodic check-ins 
 and submit progress reports. Copyright is settled case by case\, but all f
 inal outputs must be made openly and freely accessible to the public. APPL
 ICATION: a Google Form structured as a full project proposal\, plus a proj
 ect budget (percentage-based allocation is acceptable\, but be as specific
  as possible)\, a project timeline for the one-year period with key phases
 \, activities and milestones\, and a single supporting PDF containing an u
 p-to-date CV\, selected work samples\, and up to two letters of support or
  recommendation. The form is in English\; applicants who would prefer anot
 her language are invited to write to Impart to arrange an alternative. NOT
 E: the fellowship has a strict no-tolerance policy on the use of AI or LLM
 s in the application form or any submitted materials\, and any violation r
 esults in rejection. KEY DATES: applications close 31 August 2026\; awarde
 es notified December 2026\; fellowships run January to December 2027. No i
 ndividual feedback is given on unsuccessful applications.\n\nOrganisation:
  Impart in collaboration with Nalli Silks\nLocation: Remote and self-direc
 ted\; applicants may reside anywhere in the world. Projects must engage wi
 th textile practices across South Asia: Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\
 , India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
 \nAward: Up to INR 5.5 lakhs (INR 550\,000) per fellow towards project exe
 cution\, covering research-related expenses including travel\, fieldwork a
 nd documentation subject to approval. Two fellowships are awarded. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: One year\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https
 ://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote and self-directed\; applicants may reside anywhere in the w
 orld. Projects must engage with textile practices across South Asia: Afgha
 nistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pa
 kistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-public-entrepreneur-in-residence-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: New_ Public Entrepreneur in Residence\, Pilot Cohort
DESCRIPTION:New_ Public is piloting a virtual Entrepreneur in Residence pro
 gramme for solo founders building alternatives to the mainstream social pl
 atforms. Its framing: people are turning away from algorithmic social medi
 a\, AI bots are taking over the big platforms\, and users are retreating i
 nto private closed spaces\, which leaves an opening for builders working o
 n a new information ecosystem rather than the status quo. The residency su
 pplies research\, experts\, connections and tools\; the founder brings the
  vision and experience. TWO FOCUS AREAS: humans connecting to humans\, mea
 ning products that bring people into meaningful contact online (forums\, m
 essaging apps\, groups\, microblogging\, in-person meetups)\, including AI
 -powered platforms and tools as long as they connect people to people rath
 er than people to bots\; and local and civic information environments\, me
 aning tools for distributing local news\, building community knowledge and
  facilitating collective sensemaking\, so people can get trustworthy infor
 mation about their communities. WHAT RESIDENTS DO: use self-directed time 
 to prototype ideas\, test assumptions and gain clarity\; participate in re
 gular check-ins\, engage with the other resident and the wider team\, and 
 take candid feedback on product and strategy\; engage seriously with go-to
 -market questions about who the user is\, how they find you\, and the path
  to sustainability\; and help shape the programme itself\, since the pilot
  cohort's feedback directly informs how the EIR programme runs going forwa
 rd. WHO THEY WANT: entrepreneurial founders ready to build their next thin
 g within the focus areas\, with a track record of or clear drive toward wo
 rk that builds social trust\, connection and belonging\; strong product th
 inkers with good judgment\, a relentless focus on user needs\, and opennes
 s to coaching on mission-aligned business models. An early idea is enough\
 , no working product required. The pilot is open to founders with somethin
 g else going on alongside\, though the full programme will most likely be 
 full time. APPLICATION PROCESS: submit the written application online\; sh
 ortlisted applicants submit a short video by 18 September 2026 covering ba
 ckground\, motivation and a quick pitch\; full applications and videos go 
 to the advisory board\, with offers made in early October ahead of the kic
 koff. FUTURE COHORTS: New_ Public plans to open the next cohort to the UK 
 and EU from spring 2027. Deadline 11:59pm Pacific on Wednesday 9 September
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: New_ Public\nLocation: Remote\, with an in-person 
 kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (19 to 22 October 2026). Open to app
 licants based in the United States or Canada.\nAward: Stipend of USD 22\,5
 00\, paid in three monthly installments across the residency\, each contin
 gent on continued active participation. Plus USD 1\,500 toward an AI tool 
 of the resident's choice for coding\, prototyping or design. Two residents
  are selected in the pilot cohort. Also provided: hands-on product and str
 ategy support from senior New_ Public staff and outside experts\; access t
 o New_ Public's five years of original research on moderation\, governance
 \, community stewardship and platform design\, plus direct access to the r
 esearchers behind it\; and introductions across New_ Public's network of m
 ajor platforms\, startups\, university researchers and funders for hiring\
 , fundraising\, early user testing and community partnerships. Residents m
 ust submit a Form W-9 or W-8BEN to receive the stipend. No application fee
 .\nDuration: Three months\, from the week of 19 October 2026 to 29 January
  2027\, run remotely apart from the opening retreat\n\nMore: https://newpu
 blic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://newpublic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, with an in-person kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (
 19 to 22 October 2026). Open to applicants based in the United States or C
 anada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mad-artist-studios-residency-cycle-46-47@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260911
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260912
SUMMARY:Deadline: MAD Artist Studios Residency\, Cycles 46 and 47
DESCRIPTION:Established in 2008 and the first of its kind\, the Artist Stud
 ios at MAD is a public-facing community engagement and professional develo
 pment programme on the sixth floor of the Museum\, where visitors meet art
 ists-in-residence at work. Two artists are selected per six-month session 
 (the application text elsewhere says three\, so confirm with MAD). More th
 an 200 artists and designers have come through the programme\, which MAD d
 escribes as an innovative model for museum education\, with a sustained fo
 cus on artists from historically underrepresented communities whose work p
 ushes the boundaries of contemporary craft\; alumni have exhibited at the 
 Queens Museum\, Whitney\, Brooklyn Museum and Studio Museum. WHAT MAD MEAN
 S BY CRAFT: both a discipline and a way of thinking\, covering traditional
  studio craft materials such as clay\, fiber\, wood\, glass\, metal and st
 one\, and craft-based methods used in new ways\, including handwork combin
 ed with digital tools\, performance or experimental materials. Craft here 
 is defined by deep engagement with process\, materiality and the hand rath
 er than by medium. RESIDENCY EXPECTATIONS: this is a substantial public co
 mmitment\, not quiet studio time. Residents commit to 14 or 21 public open
  studio hours a week on Thursdays\, Fridays and/or Saturdays 10:30am to 5:
 30pm and/or Wednesdays 11:00am to 7:00pm\, on a schedule fixed with MAD's 
 Education Department for the whole six months. During those hours resident
 s host K-12 school groups (in person and virtual) and public tour groups. 
 Because of the contact with schoolchildren\, all selected artists must pas
 s a background check. ELIGIBILITY: an artist or designer with an active st
 udio practice in the applied arts\; a resident of the New York City metro 
 area\, defined as the five boroughs plus nearby New York\, New Jersey and 
 Connecticut counties within reasonable commuting distance\, typically on M
 TA\, PATH or NJ Transit\; at least 21 at the time of application\; and a U
 S citizen or legal US resident with a valid SSN or EIN. The Museum does no
 t sponsor visas and states this is not subject to exception. Programme alu
 mni may not reapply\, though unsuccessful past applicants may. SELECTION: 
 the committee looks for a mature body of work engaging critically with the
  transdisciplinary fields of contemporary craft\, design\, art and technol
 ogy\, a strong conceptual framework\, deep engagement with process and mat
 erials\, a clear direction or question for the residency\, and genuine rea
 diness for the open studios format. Top applications go to interview with 
 the Deputy Director of Education and Manager of Public Programs and Studio
 s\, and finalists' references are contacted. Applicants are strongly encou
 raged to visit the studios in person first. NOT AN EXHIBITION PROGRAMME: r
 esidents may show work in progress in their studio and finished work in th
 e sixth-floor vitrines\, but the residency does not include a gallery exhi
 bition or a proposal for MAD's galleries. STUDIO RESTRICTIONS: no open fla
 mes\, all hot work approved by the Director of Facilities\, no large or un
 safe power tools such as saws\, and no toxic materials including turpentin
 e\, certain resins\, glazes\, lusters and lead-based paints. Applicants su
 bmit one application indicating their preferred schedule and which session
  or sessions they want\, and are selected for one. Initial notifications O
 ctober 2026\, finalist interviews November 2026\, offers December 2026. Qu
 estions: education@madmuseum.org. Deadline 11 September 2026.\n\nOrganisat
 ion: Museum of Arts and Design (MAD)\nLocation: Sixth floor of the Museum 
 of Arts and Design\, New York City\, USA. Open to residents of the New Yor
 k City metro area only.\nAward: Stipend based on the on-site public schedu
 le: USD 15\,000 for three public-facing days per week\, or USD 10\,000 for
  two days. Also provided: a dedicated studio of roughly 200 square feet in
  the Museum with worktable\, sink\, storage and Wi-Fi\; a separate stipend
  for materials and mentorship\; professional development and studio visits
  with museum staff\; additional paid public programming opportunities\; ad
 mission to paid public programmes\; and access to MAD's galleries\, public
  programmes and exhibition openings. Shared equipment includes a Skutt 102
 7 kiln\, a Brent pottery wheel and a 22 inch floor loom. MAD does not pay 
 travel costs or provide housing. Residents are one-time independent contra
 ctors and are responsible for their own taxes. Collectives may apply but a
 re paid the individual stipend. APPLICATION FEE: USD 5 per application\, w
 ith fee waivers available on request by emailing education@madmuseum.org b
 efore submitting.\nDuration: Six months. Cycle 46 runs 15 February 2027 to
  15 August 2027\; Cycle 47 runs 16 August 2027 to 13 February 2028.\n\nMor
 e: https://madmuseum.submittable.com/submit/363177/mad-artist-studios-arti
 st-residency-cycle-46-and-47?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
URL:https://madmuseum.submittable.com/submit/363177/mad-artist-studios-arti
 st-residency-cycle-46-and-47?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Sixth floor of the Museum of Arts and Design\, New York City\, USA
 . Open to residents of the New York City metro area only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:adagp-sgdl-arcane-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: Arcane Grant 2026 for Artist Books
DESCRIPTION:Artists' books get little support\, financially or in distribut
 ion\, despite their place in art history and in the careers of the people 
 who make them. ADAGP\, the French collecting society for copyright in the 
 visual arts\, and SGDL\, the French society of authors\, created the Arcan
 e grant in response: EUR 9\,000 to one winning project for the conception 
 and production of an artist's book made collaboratively by a visual artist
  and a writer. WHO CAN APPLY: a pair consisting of a professional visual a
 rtist from the French scene (working or residing in France for at least fi
 ve years\, or a French national living abroad) and a French-language autho
 r working in fiction\, non-fiction\, poetry\, children's literature or the
 atre who has already been published by a publisher. A trio is also possibl
 e where a publishing structure joins the project. WHAT COUNTS AS AN ARTIST
 'S BOOK: a creation\, a work of art in book form\, in a limited print run 
 of between 1 and 500 copies\, which may be signed or numbered. Explicitly 
 not eligible: magazines\, fanzines\, limited editions of an ordinary book\
 , and any publication that simply presents an artist's work\, such as mono
 graphs or illustrated books. APPLICATION: the file must be in French and i
 ncludes a jointly written project presentation of up to three pages coveri
 ng the artistic and editorial vision\, constituent elements\, methods of r
 ealisation and desired final form\; a one-page technical sheet giving titl
 e\, print run\, format\, page count\, paper quality\, printing techniques 
 and materials\; a provisional timetable\; and a detailed balanced provisio
 nal budget of expenses and financing. Artistic elements are a CV of exhibi
 tions and publications (2 pages) and a portfolio with website link (10 pag
 es) for the artist\, a CV and website link for the author (2 pages)\, and 
 a one-page editorial line for the publishing structure if there is one\; c
 ontact details must be omitted from these. Administrative elements are swo
 rn declarations that the project is a new creation with copyright held sol
 ely by the pair\, that the artist is from the French scene\, and\, if appl
 icable\, that the publishing structure undertakes to publish the work. Tem
 plates are downloadable from the Required Documents page. TIMELINE: call l
 aunched 7 July 2026\, applications close 7 September 2026 at 2pm\, jury me
 ets November 2026\, winner announced end of November 2026\, and the work m
 ust be published within two years of that announcement. The regulations mu
 st be read before applying\, and submitting an application constitutes acc
 eptance of them. Contact: arcane@adagp.fr.\n\nOrganisation: ADAGP and SGDL
  (Societe des Gens de Lettres)\nLocation: France. Open to a professional v
 isual artist from the French scene (working or residing in France for at l
 east five years\, or of French nationality living abroad) paired with a Fr
 ench-language author.\nAward: EUR 9\,000 to the single winning project\, s
 upporting the conception and production of an artist's book. NOTE ON TAX: 
 ADAGP grants count as primary artistic income under the French Intellectua
 l Property Code (art. L112-2)\, so the sum falls under the artist-author s
 ocial security and tax regime\, subject to URSSAF contributions and income
  tax declared as non-commercial profits (micro-BNC or controlled declarati
 on). VAT is not due. No application fee.\nDuration: The winning duo or tri
 o commits to publishing the artist's book within two years of the announce
 ment\n\nMore: https://candidatures.adagp.fr/en/?option=com_emundus&view=pr
 ogramme&cid=187&Itemid=1531&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://candidatures.adagp.fr/en/?option=com_emundus&view=programme&cid
 =187&Itemid=1531&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:France. Open to a professional visual artist from the French scene
  (working or residing in France for at least five years\, or of French nat
 ionality living abroad) paired with a French-language author.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:next-art-ai-award-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI ART AWARD: NEXT ART AI 2026
DESCRIPTION:NEXT ART AI is an open AI art competition run by the HOBAN Cult
 ural Foundation\, welcoming participants of all backgrounds regardless of 
 career\, nationality or age\, and framed as a way to reflect on the future
  of AI art through new modes of expression and artistic experimentation. T
 WO CATEGORIES: AI IMAGE ART\, for works in a single-image format at 150dpi
  or higher\, submitted as JPG\, JPEG\, PNG or BMP up to 10MB\; and AI VIDE
 O ART\, open to a wide range of video formats including animation\, motion
  graphics and live-action footage enhanced or transformed using AI\, at 10
 80p or higher\, under one minute\, 30 or 60fps recommended\, stereo 48kHz 
 audio recommended\, as mp4 (h.264) or mov. Video entries are submitted as 
 a URL via YouTube\, Vimeo\, Dropbox or OneDrive\, or an alternative platfo
 rm if those are restricted in your region\; links that are inaccessible du
 e to privacy settings may be excluded from judging. WHO CAN ENTER: individ
 uals or teams of up to three including the representative\, though you may
  not submit both as an individual and as part of a team. Team representati
 ves create the account in their own name and all members submit a consent 
 form for personal data. Applicants under 19 need a guardian's signed conse
 nt. One work may be submitted per category. RULES ON AI AND ASSETS: work m
 ust be original and created by the applicant or team\, and must be image o
 r video art created using generative AI platforms. Works that have previou
 sly won an award in another competition are not eligible. Free or paid ext
 ernal assets\, game engines\, AI frameworks\, open-source tools and editin
 g software may be used freely\, but applicants must hold licences permitti
 ng commercial use for every element\, and work infringing third-party righ
 ts is excluded. Even where external assets and editing software are used\,
  the creative process must involve AI\, whether AI-assisted direction\, tr
 ansformation\, generation or other AI-based processes\, and the choice of 
 AI tools\, the scope and method of their use\, and the prompts themselves 
 are all weighed in evaluating the creative use of AI. TIMELINE: applicatio
 ns open 1 September 2026 and close 30 September 2026 at 11:59pm KST\, prel
 iminary round early October\, final round late October\, announcement and 
 ceremony early November\, exhibition late November 2026. Organised and hos
 ted by the HOBAN Cultural Foundation\, sponsored by HOBAN Group\, with Seo
 ul Shinmun and EBN as media partners. Details and schedule are subject to 
 change at the organiser's discretion.\n\nOrganisation: HOBAN Cultural Foun
 dation\nLocation: Online submission\, with the awards ceremony and exhibit
 ion in South Korea. Open to all regardless of career\, nationality or age.
 \nAward: Total prize pool of KRW 21\,000\,000 across 20 winners\, paid aft
 er deduction of applicable taxes and public charges. Grand Prize KRW 5\,00
 0\,000 (selected regardless of category)\; in each of the two categories\,
  First Prize KRW 2\,000\,000\, three Second Prizes of KRW 1\,000\,000 each
 \, and five Honorable Finalist awards of KRW 500\,000 each\; plus a HOBAN 
 Group President's Award of KRW 1\,000\,000. Winners also receive plaques a
 nd certificates\, online and offline exhibition opportunities\, and a feat
 ure on the official online platform. No entry fee is mentioned.\nDuration:
  One-off competition\; exhibition late November 2026\n\nMore: https://next
 -art-ai.com/en/apply/overview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://next-art-ai.com/en/apply/overview?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online submission\, with the awards ceremony and exhibition in Sou
 th Korea. Open to all regardless of career\, nationality or age.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:epic-megagrants-cycle-2-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260904
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260905
SUMMARY:Deadline: Epic MegaGrants 2026 (Cycle 2)
DESCRIPTION:Epic MegaGrants are financial grants from Epic Games for creati
 ve\, noteworthy and innovative projects built in and around Unreal Engine\
 , or projects that enhance the open source 3D graphics ecosystem. Over 2\,
 000 grants have been awarded to date. The programme is explicitly aimed at
  smaller teams\, solo developers and individuals as well as studios: Epic 
 states that individuals and small teams are eligible and encouraged\, and 
 that it treats every project equally regardless of who you are. WHAT IS FU
 NDED: (1) INDIE GAMES\, small or solo teams working in Unreal Engine\, sub
 mitted past the idea and design phase with at least some working prototype
 \, ideally with a video link\; (2) UEFN EXPERIENCES\, Fortnite Creative is
 lands and interactive experiences built in Unreal Editor for Fortnite\; (3
 ) ENGINE MIGRATION\, a proportion of MegaGrants is reserved for teams movi
 ng a partially developed project from another engine to Unreal\; (4) FILM\
 , TV AND LIVE EVENTS\, including live-action and animated films and shows\
 , broadcast\, live and location-based events\; (5) OTHER NON-GAMES VERTICA
 LS such as architecture\, automotive\, manufacturing\, simulation\, produc
 t design\, advertising and VR/AR\, where more detailed project plans and p
 rototypes are expected\; (6) EDUCATION\, including research\, curricula an
 d course development and university programmes\, though for institutions E
 pic accepts submissions only from degree-granting schools and educators at
  such schools\; (7) TOOLS AND OPEN SOURCE\, creating open source tools or 
 enhancing open source software using Unreal Engine. NON-UNREAL PROJECTS AR
 E ELIGIBLE if you want to move them to Unreal Engine\, or if the project e
 nhances open source 3D content creation whether or not it relates to Unrea
 l Engine. EVALUATION rests on three things: quality and unique appeal (hig
 h-quality demos or builds\, well-documented descriptions\, technical ambit
 ion)\; community benefit (how you will give back\, for example open-sourci
 ng the project\, releasing assets through a marketplace\, sharing learning
 s\, or shipping an SDK\, tool or plug-in)\; and viability (a clear budget 
 with major spend areas and a project plan with milestones\, deliverables a
 nd timeline). NOTE ON DEADLINES: the FAQ still describes MegaGrants as an 
 ongoing programme with no specific deadline\, but the timeline published o
 n the same page now runs in fixed cycles\, and the Cycle 2 submission wind
 ow closes 4 September 2026 at 23:59 ET. Treat the cycle date as the operat
 ive deadline. You may apply for more than one project\, and may reapply fo
 r the same project after a decision if you have made updates. Epic is not 
 offering sponsorships and cannot give feedback on applications. Apply at m
 egagrants.unrealengine.com.\n\nOrganisation: Epic Games\nLocation: Open wo
 rldwide. Epic states that if it can legally make payment to you\, you are 
 eligible.\nAward: Typical grants range from USD 5\,000 to USD 75\,000\, wi
 th some exceptional projects receiving up to USD 150\,000. Epic states tha
 t recipients continue to own their IP and may publish it however they wish
 \, so this is a grant rather than an investment or equity deal. Grants up 
 to and including USD 25\,000 may be paid in a single instalment\; larger g
 rants are paid across several instalments tied to continued progress. Fund
 s are for project and team expenses such as development and hiring\; hardw
 are purchases or rentals are approved only occasionally\, case by case. No
  application fee.\nDuration: Cycle 2 of 2026 runs 29 June to 11 December 2
 026. Submission window 29 June to 4 September 2026 (closes 23:59 ET)\, rev
 iew 5 September to 4 December 2026\, notices 7 to 11 December 2026. Cycle 
 1 ran 12 January to 19 June 2026.\n\nMore: https://www.unrealengine.com/me
 gagrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.unrealengine.com/megagrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Open worldwide. Epic states that if it can legally make payment to
  you\, you are eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:africa-no-filter-voices-for-climate-justice-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: Africa No Filter: Voices for Climate Justice 2026
DESCRIPTION:Voices for Climate Justice supports African storytellers making
  work about how climate finance reaches the continent as debt rather than 
 as grants. The framing of the call: African nations are being pushed furth
 er into debt through loans despite the continent's minimal contribution to
  the crisis\, with many governments spending more on debt repayment than o
 n health and climate adaptation combined. WHO IT IS FOR: journalists\, con
 tent creators\, podcasters\, vloggers\, muralists and data visualizers. WH
 AT YOU GET: a 12-month programme delivered with Comic Relief giving 15 to 
 20 storytellers training in climate justice\, data storytelling and narrat
 ive framing\, then mentorship and work with researchers and partners to de
 velop accurate\, research-based stories\, plus content amplification and g
 rants of up to USD 4\,000. ELIGIBILITY: aged 18 or over\; based in one of 
 the ten listed countries\; an active and highly engaged platform (podcast\
 , YouTube channel\, TikTok\, Facebook\, Instagram\, publication or data vi
 sualization portfolio) running for more than a year\; a combined audience 
 of 5\,000 or more followers across Instagram\, TikTok\, Facebook\, Twitter
 /X and Snapchat for content creators and data visualizers\, while journali
 sts may instead demonstrate reach through publication circulation\; conten
 t creators must be unsigned and not represented by an agent\, though journ
 alists may be affiliated with a publication provided the submitted work an
 d content rights remain their own\; a commitment to publishing at least fo
 ur pieces on gender\, debt and fossil fuels over at least six months\; and
  individual applicants only\, no organisations or agencies. Proposals must
  focus on the campaign's core themes of climate justice\, gender\, debt an
 d fossil fuels in Africa. NOT CONSIDERED: incomplete applications\, postal
  or email submissions\, hacked material\, vulgar\, racist\, sexist or homo
 phobic material\, work reinforcing stereotypes of Africa as poor\, corrupt
 \, diseased\, conflict-ridden or lacking agency\, and accounts or posts bo
 osted by paid engagement. HOW TO APPLY: prepare a pitch deck of no more th
 an 5 slides or a video of no more than 60 seconds connecting gender\, debt
  and fossil fuels in Africa\, then submit through the ANF application port
 al with links to your platforms and a short description of the audience yo
 u plan to reach. Content may be delivered in English or French. Selection 
 weighs originality of the idea\, relevance to ANF's mission of storytellin
 g beyond stereotypes\, relevance to the gender-debt-fossil fuel focus\, an
 d reach or standing on your platform or in your community. Only shortliste
 d candidates are notified.\n\nOrganisation: Africa No Filter\, in partners
 hip with Comic Relief\nLocation: Applicants must be based in Mozambique\, 
 Uganda\, Nigeria\, Angola\, Chad\, the Republic of Congo\, Tanzania\, Sout
 h Sudan\, Malawi or Zambia.\nAward: Grants of up to USD 4\,000 (the call s
 tates a range of USD 0 to 4\,000)\, alongside training\, mentorship and co
 ntent amplification. Between 15 and 20 storytellers will be supported. No 
 application fee.\nDuration: 12-month programme. Applications opened 11 Aug
 ust 2026 and are reviewed on a rolling basis\; deadline 25 September 2026 
 at 18:00 GMT. Screening 5 October\, interviews 9 October\, outcomes commun
 icated in the first week of October and grantees announced 15 October 2026
 .\n\nMore: https://africanofilter.org/what-we-do/community/voices-for-clim
 ate-justice/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://africanofilter.org/what-we-do/community/voices-for-climate-just
 ice/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicants must be based in Mozambique\, Uganda\, Nigeria\, Angola
 \, Chad\, the Republic of Congo\, Tanzania\, South Sudan\, Malawi or Zambi
 a.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:le-garage-moderne-oasis-residencies-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Le Garage Moderne: OASIS International Artist Residencies
  2026 (Bordeaux)
DESCRIPTION:Second year of Le Garage Moderne's residency cycle within the E
 uropean OASIS project\, offering five grants to international artists for 
 two-week stays in Bordeaux. THE SITE IS THE POINT. Le Garage Moderne is a 
 working non-profit car and bicycle workshop with a neighbourhood canteen a
 nd a versatile venue for performing arts\, concerts and community gatherin
 gs\, all at once a productive space\, a public space and a community-drive
 n common. Having just moved back into its 19th-century building after a lo
 ng renovation\, the team describes machines and workbenches back in place 
 and a brand-new playground\, and invites residents to take over the space 
 and explore its physical and social possibilities. They ask for SITE-SPECI
 FIC PROPOSALS that play with the verticality\, the scale of the space\, th
 e community and what they call the hive-like vibe of the venue\, where eve
 ryone runs around\, shouts\, gets their hands dirty and learns in the proc
 ess. DISCIPLINES: any\, explicitly including design\, visual art\, perform
 ing arts\, film\, small-scale architecture\, emergency architecture\, craf
 t-making\, street furniture\, sound\, and interdisciplinary or community-b
 ased practices. Proposals combining artistic creation with the spatial and
  the social are especially encouraged\, and the organisers say plainly tha
 t they want to be surprised. WHAT IS EXPECTED: openness to exchange and co
 llaboration with the 11 permanent residents and with 3 local artists recru
 ited through a separate OASIS call\; participation in 2 days of masterclas
 s open to members and community\, where you describe and share your practi
 ce\, technique and working context with fellow OASIS residents\, local art
 ists\, members and staff\; and a public outcome\, which need not be a larg
 e event but must be something the public can experience. RIGHTS: work prod
 uced during the residency remains the artist's property\, but documentatio
 n of it is made by Le Garage Moderne and will be publicised by them for th
 ree years after the residency. Note this is a different OASIS partner call
  from the OASis In the Park (Naples) and Disappearing Landscape (High Tatr
 as) rounds under the same European project.\n\nOrganisation: Le Garage Mod
 erne (European OASIS project)\nLocation: Bacalan district\, Bordeaux\, Fra
 nce. Le Garage Moderne is a non-profit car garage and bicycle workshop\, a
  canteen serving sponsored meals\, and a cultural venue housing 11 permane
 nt artists\, makers and craftswomen\, recently returned to its 19th-centur
 y building after a two-and-a-half-year renovation.\nAward: Five grants. Ea
 ch artist receives a fee of EUR 1\,300 gross (taxes may apply depending on
  legal status)\, up to EUR 600 for production expenses such as raw materia
 ls\, technical services or special tools\, a lump-sum travel grant scaled 
 to distance with top-ups for green travel options\, a per diem of EUR 45 p
 er day\, and accommodation in a self-catering hotel. Access to the shared 
 tools and spaces of Le Garage Moderne is included\, subject to the associa
 tion's regulations. No application fee.\nDuration: Two weeks on site. Five
  overlapping two-week slots\, and applicants may choose one or more: 9 to 
 22 November\, 16 to 29 November\, 23 November to 6 December\, 30 November 
 to 13 December\, and 7 to 20 December 2026. Deadline 1 September 2026\; pr
 e-selection interviews 15 September\; final selection announced 1 October 
 2026.\n\nMore: https://legaragemoderne.org/oasis2026?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://legaragemoderne.org/oasis2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Bacalan district\, Bordeaux\, France. Le Garage Moderne is a non-p
 rofit car garage and bicycle workshop\, a canteen serving sponsored meals\
 , and a cultural venue housing 11 permanent artists\, makers and craftswom
 en\, recently returned to its 19th-century building after a two-and-a-half
 -year renovation.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:futures-of-listening-architecture-on-sound-2027@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Futures of Listening: Architecture on Sound Residency
DESCRIPTION:One residency for an individual practitioner\, a duo or a studi
 o working at the intersection of sound and space\, exploring architecture 
 through spatial audio. THE PREMISE: architecture is traditionally conceive
 d through form\, structure\, material and light\, with sound treated as so
 mething to be controlled or optimised afterwards. This residency asks what
  happens if architecture begins with sound\, shifting the question from ho
 w a space should sound to how sound can generate space. Spatial audio\, vi
 rtual acoustics and immersive media now allow prototyping of responsive en
 vironments\, impossible architectures and sonic spaces beyond physical con
 straints. RESEARCH QUESTIONS include how sound can become a first principl
 e in architectural and spatial design\, how the acoustic properties of spa
 ce inform the creation and perception of spatial sound\, and how artistic 
 research can shape the future tools\, interfaces and methods of spatial so
 und creation. NOT JUST ARTWORKS: they explicitly say they want new methods
 \, insights and creative infrastructures too. Outcomes may include install
 ations\, spatial audio compositions\, performances\, audiovisual works\, s
 oftware prototypes\, reusable methodologies or modular interfaces. Residen
 ts may collaborate with a 4DSOUND software developer to prototype new tool
 s\, and practitioners who already build their own software\, hardware or c
 reative technologies are encouraged to advance those as part of the reside
 ncy. WHO: architects are the named invitees\, with scenographers\, spatial
  designers\, installation artists\, sound artists\, composers and creative
  technologists also encouraged. Creative coding experience is welcome but 
 not required. Lead researcher is Poul Holleman\, co-founder and director o
 f 4DSOUND. HARD ELIGIBILITY GATE: applicants must be based in the Netherla
 nds AND registered as an artist with the Dutch Chamber of Commerce (KvK). 
 This is not negotiable and rules out non-NL applicants. Futures of Listeni
 ng is a Netherlands-based platform for spatial sound\, immersive experienc
 e\, performance and installation art\, founded by FIBER Festival\, 4DSOUND
 \, Robin Koek and Art of Sound at the Royal Conservatoire The Hague\, supp
 orted by CIIIC and Stimuleringsfonds.\n\nOrganisation: Futures of Listenin
 g (FIBER\, 4DSOUND\, Robin Koek\, Art of Sound)\nLocation: Amsterdam\, Net
 herlands\, with work at the 4DSOUND studio.\nAward: Total residency fee of
  EUR 7\,125\, calculated as 25 commissioned days at a day rate of EUR 285.
  Six of those 25 days can be spent at the 4DSOUND studio working with thei
 r team. Some additional financial support is available if needed\, includi
 ng material and travel costs\, but these must be clearly defined and agree
 d before the residency starts. No application fee.\nDuration: December 202
 6 to February 2027\, structured as 25 commissioned days. Deadline 13 Septe
 mber 2026.\n\nMore: https://fiber.homerun.co/artist-residency-architecture
 -on-sound/en?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://fiber.homerun.co/artist-residency-architecture-on-sound/en?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Amsterdam\, Netherlands\, with work at the 4DSOUND studio.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:futures-of-listening-worlding-narratives-roaming-sound-space-2027@artif
 icialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Futures of Listening: Worlding Narratives\, Roaming Sound
  Space Residency
DESCRIPTION:One residency to research and develop an interactive listening 
 experience built on non-linear sound narratives\, experienced by navigatin
 g through a series of virtual spaces or layers. NO VISUALS: Worlding Narra
 tives centres sound as the primary medium for immersion and intentionally 
 removes dominant visual layers such as VR\, AR\, projection or video. THE 
 DESIGN PROBLEM they want tackled is translation and adaptation\, specifica
 lly the shift from one visitor in a headphone-mediated experience to rende
 ring for a multi-speaker system for group interaction. That change in dyna
 mics\, reproduction and perspective is integral to the residency scope. RE
 SEARCH QUESTIONS include how immersive sound can function as a primary nar
 rative medium across speculative and non-fictional realities\, what new na
 rrative structures and dramaturgical methods become possible when the crea
 tive process originates from sound rather than adapting frameworks from ot
 her media\, and how interactive sonic structures can create navigable list
 ening experiences designed for and by multiple intelligences. Outcomes may
  include interactive sonic prototypes\, spatial audio compositions\, immer
 sive listening experiences\, narrative sound works or software prototypes.
  WORTH NOTING: your residency runs in tandem with resident Pinar Temiz\, a
 n expert in sound design for gaming environments\, with the opportunity to
  work together. Lead researcher is Dr Angela McArthur of UCL\, with resear
 ch support from Dr meLe yamomo of the University of Amsterdam. WHO: media 
 and sound artists\, sound designers\, musicians producing their own sonic 
 work\, field recordists\, writers\, composers and game sound designers. Ex
 perience with interactive audio\, spatial sound tools or game-sound workfl
 ows is welcome but not required. HARD ELIGIBILITY GATE: applicants must be
  based in the Netherlands AND registered as an artist with the Dutch Chamb
 er of Commerce (KvK). This is not negotiable and rules out non-NL applican
 ts. Futures of Listening is a Netherlands-based platform for spatial sound
 \, immersive experience\, performance and installation art\, founded by FI
 BER Festival\, 4DSOUND\, Robin Koek and Art of Sound at the Royal Conserva
 toire The Hague\, supported by CIIIC and Stimuleringsfonds.\n\nOrganisatio
 n: Futures of Listening (FIBER\, 4DSOUND\, Robin Koek\, Art of Sound)\nLoc
 ation: Amsterdam\, Netherlands\, with remote and studio access to the 4DSO
 UND infrastructure.\nAward: Total residency fee of EUR 7\,125\, calculated
  as 25 commissioned days at a day rate of EUR 285. Six of those 25 days ca
 n be spent at the 4DSOUND studio working with their team. Some additional 
 financial support is available if needed\, including material and travel c
 osts\, but these must be clearly defined and agreed before the residency s
 tarts. No application fee.\nDuration: January to March 2027\, structured a
 s 25 commissioned days\, running in tandem with fellow Worlding Narratives
  resident Pinar Temiz. Deadline 13 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://fiber.
 homerun.co/artist-residency-worlding-narratives/en?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://fiber.homerun.co/artist-residency-worlding-narratives/en?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Amsterdam\, Netherlands\, with remote and studio access to the 4DS
 OUND infrastructure.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
